  "shared_rationale": {
    "financing": "Illustrative assumptions, not a financing offer. Common supported external terms: 8% interest, 4-year grace and 9-year maturity. Held constant across this case set; this comparison does not test alternative financing.",
    "20": "Illustrative assumed growth increment of 0.2 percentage points in 2030–2034, zero elsewhere. This is not a calibrated investment effect or forecast. When growth returns to baseline, the higher GDP level persists in the assumption. Spending and grant-ratio paths were jointly mapped to this growth path; editing growth alone does not preserve that fiscal interpretation. Use the complete alternative case or remap the linked paths.",
    "13": "Illustrative investment costs 0.5% of each year’s baseline GDP in 2027–2029, followed by 0.05% maintenance annually 2030–2044. Other primary spending retains its already-planned baseline level; it is not reduced below that path. Lower ratios reflect the larger GDP denominator. This does not establish service quality or spending per person. Figures entered here are the complete mapped ratio adjustments, including the investment. Interest is not part of primary spending.",
    "11": "Revenue excluding grants retains its baseline GDP share. Existing grants retain baseline nominal amounts, so the matching adjustment to revenue-and-grants avoids inventing new grant funding. Additional non-grant receipts from higher output are assumed, not independently modelled. Statutory tax rates are not identified.",
    "15": "Existing grants remain at their baseline nominal amounts. Their GDP share falls when the assumed GDP level rises. The same share adjustment is included in revenue-and-grants to preserve the non-grant revenue share. No additional grant finance is assumed.",
    "30": "Imported inputs amount to 0.2% of each year’s baseline GDP in 2027–2029, within the 0.5% investment cost, not an additional fiscal cost. Other imports retain their baseline GDP share. This is a simplified illustrative external-response assumption."
